// THE BOOK OF GATES IN PLAIN WORDS: a close paraphrase of each passage of
// Budge's English (The Egyptian Heaven and Hell, vol. II, 1905), written for
// this archive. Budge follows the alabaster sarcophagus of Seti I and fills
// its gaps from the royal tombs; his sections mix his own description of the
// scene with his translation of the texts beside it, and the paraphrase keeps
// the two apart ("Budge describes…", "The text says…"). Long lists of names
// are given only in summary. Where Budge marks a reading as doubtful ("(?)",
// "or, …") and the sense turns on it, the paraphrase ends with "Other
// possible readings".

export const PLAIN_BOOK_OF_GATES: Record<string, string> = {
  // DIVISION 1 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.1.opening":
    "Budge describes the first scene on Seti I's sarcophagus: the western horizon, the mountain of the west, split in two, with the sun's boat sailing between the halves into the underworld. On each side stands a standard, one jackal-headed and one ram-headed, with two kneeling gods beside it, Tat and Set, personifying the region beyond the day and the funeral mountain. In the boat the sun is a beetle in a disk wrapped in the coils of a serpent with its tail in its mouth; the god Sa (divine intelligence) stands in the bows and Heka (magic) at the steering oars.\n\nThe text: Ra says to the mountain: send out light, mountain; let radiance rise from what devoured me, which has killed men and is full of the slaughter of gods. Breath to you who live in light; my splendour will be yours. I have hidden you from those on earth. The gods say: let this jackal sceptre speak the words of the great god who joins his limbs together; come to us, you from whom we came; cries of joy to you in your disk, great god of many forms. Their food is bread and beer. Budge leaves out a second, almost identical paragraph.",
  "egy.gates.1.upper":
    "On one side of the boat stand twelve 'gods of the mountain'. The text says they came into being from Ra, from his substance, and came out of his eye. He has given them as their home the hidden mountain, which consumes men, gods, all cattle and all reptiles that the great god has made; he has decided its plans after making them spring up in the earth he created.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'a place (or, abode)' and 'plans (or, designs)' are Budge's own alternatives.",
  "egy.gates.1.lower":
    "On the other side of the boat stand twelve 'gods of Set-Amentet', the mountain of the West. The text: the hidden place. These are the ones who have consumed men, gods, all cattle and all reptiles that the great god has made. He has decided plans for them after making them spring up in the land he created, that is, in the West which he made.",

  // DIVISION 2 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.2.gate":
    "Budge describes the first gate. Ra's boat, having passed between the two halves of the western horizon, comes to a closed door. Unlike the later doors it has a single leaf turning on a pivot. A serpent, Saa-set, standing on its tail, guards it.\n\nThe text: the one who is over this door opens it to Ra. Sa says to the serpent: open your door to Ra, throw it wide to Khuti (the god of the horizon); the hidden home is dark, so that this god's changes of form may take place. The door shuts after the god has gone in, and those in their mountain lament when they hear it close.",
  "egy.gates.2.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the second division. Four gods tow Ra's boat. Ra is a ram-headed man with the sun's disk, standing in a shrine wrapped in the coils of the serpent Mehen, with Sa in front and Heka behind. A company of thirteen gods, led by a god with a staff, meets the boat.\n\nThe text: the gods of the underworld tow the great god along its roads so that he may make divisions in the earth, carry out his plans, weigh words and deeds in the West, set the blessed dead on their thrones and send the damned to the place they were sentenced to in judgment, destroying their bodies by an evil death. Ra says: let me restore the crown and possess my shrine in the earth; let Sa and Heka join me to plan for you; what is yours is yours; Isis has calmed the wind; no one shuts the door on you and the damned do not come in after you. The gods answer: the road is dark, so let the closed doors open and the earth part, so that the gods may tow their creator. Their food is funeral offerings and their drink cool water.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'divisions (or, distinctions)' and 'attributes (or, forms)' are Budge's own alternatives.",
  "egy.gates.2.upper":
    "On one side of the boat stand twenty-four gods: twelve 'worshippers of Ra who are at peace' and twelve 'righteous who are in the underworld'. Of the first the text says: they praised Ra on earth, spoke spells against Apep, made him offerings and burned incense to their gods. They have their cool waters and their meat, and eat their offerings at the gateway of the one whose name is hidden. Ra tells them: your offerings are yours, your souls will never be hacked in pieces, your meat will never fail, you who praised me and defeated Apep for me.\n\nOf the righteous it says: they spoke truth on earth and did not think evil of the gods. They live on truth (maat), and their cool water is in their cisterns. Ra tells them: truth is yours; you yourselves are truth. Their cool waters are waters of fire to the guilty and sinful. They say to Ra: may the disk be firm, may the one in the shrine rule it, and may the serpent guard him well.",
  "egy.gates.2.lower":
    "On the other side stand the aged god Tem, leaning on a stick; four men lying on their backs, 'the inert'; and twenty men bent forward with their arms tied at the elbows behind them, 'the rebels of the hall of Ra', who cursed Ra on earth, called evil down on the one in the Egg, pushed aside what was right and spoke against Khuti.\n\nThe text: Tem works for Ra, praises him and hands out punishments to his enemies. He says: the word of my father Ra is true against you, and so is mine. I am the son who comes from his father and the father who comes from his son. You are tied with strong cords by my decree; your arms will never be opened. Your evil deeds, plots and disgusting acts have come back on you; your fate is decreed before Ra; your crooked judgments and curses fall on yourselves. You sinned in the great hall; your bodies will be cut in pieces, your souls will not exist, and you will never again see Ra. Hail Ra; your enemies are in the place of destruction.",

  // DIVISION 3 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.3.gate":
    "Budge describes the second gate, which is fortified. An outer wall guarded by nine mummy-shaped gods, 'the second company of the gods', has an opening into a corridor between two walls topped with pointed stakes. A mummy god stands at each end of the corridor, holding out his hands to Ra. At the corners two fire-breathing serpents send their flames along the corridor; the flames are 'for Ra'. The gateway is called Septet-uauau, and its inward-opening door is guarded by the upright serpent Aqebi.\n\nThe text: when the god comes to this gateway, the gods in it cry: let this gateway open to Khuti and the doors to the one in heaven; come, traveller in the West. Sa says to Aqebi: open your gate to Ra; he will light up the darkness and force a way for light into the hidden home. The door closes after him, and those in the gateway lament when they hear it.",
  "egy.gates.3.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the third division. Four gods tow Ra's boat by a rope fixed to the ends of a strange long beam with a bull's head at each end, 'his boat'; since the towing gods appear again at its far end, the boat must pass through it. Eight mummy gods carry the beam, a bull stands at each end, and seven gods sit on it. Four mummies with crossed arms stand at the end of the division.\n\nThe text: the great god comes to the 'boat of the earth', the bark of the gods. Ra greets those who carry it: strength to your forms and light to your bark; holy is the one in the boat of the earth; I travel into the hidden home to carry out its plans. A god says: praised be the soul that the Double Bull has swallowed. When Ra has passed through, the gods at the far end say: praised be Ra, whose soul the earth god has absorbed. They live on the plants of the year. Then the gods address the wrapped ones whose arms are hidden: air to your nostrils, may your wrappings burst open, may you rule your meat and have peace in what I have made. They eat bread of red grain, drink cool water, and are fed because their clothing is white in the underworld.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'peace (or, rest)', 'stand (?)' and 'the gods of the Tuat (?)' are marked by Budge as alternatives or doubtful.",
  "egy.gates.3.upper":
    "Budge describes one side of the third division: twelve shrines with open doors, each holding a mummy god, 'the holy gods in the underworld', guarded by a huge serpent called Seti; and a long lake of boiling water in which twelve gods with black heads and white bodies or clothes stand up to their waists, each with an ear of wheat before him.\n\nThe text about the shrines: Ra says: open your shrines so that my light may reach your darkness; I found you weeping with your shrines shut tight; you will have air and plenty. They say: hail Ra, come into our lake. They live on bread, drink of red barley and cool water, which the fiery serpent Seti gives them. They cry out in grief when their doors close after he has passed.\n\nThe text about the lake: it is surrounded by clothed gods with bare heads and filled with green plants. Its water is boiling; birds fly off when they see it and smell its stench. Ra tells its gods: you who guard the green plants of your lake, air to your nostrils; its water will be yours, but it will not be boiling for you, nor will its heat touch your bodies. They answer: come, you whose eye is a blazing fire with a pupil that gives light. They eat bread and green plants and drink beer of kemtet plants.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge's own alternatives here are 'drink (or, beer)' and, for the gods' white appearance, either white bodies or white clothing.",
  "egy.gates.3.lower":
    "Budge describes the other side of the third division: in two scenes, the aged god Tem leaning on a staff. Before the first lies the great serpent Apep in huge coils, with nine men behind it, 'the officials who drive back Apep'. Before the second stand nine gods with life signs and sceptres, 'the lords of destinies'.\n\nThe text: Tem has protected Ra with spells and overthrown the serpent Seba. He says: you are down and will never rise; you are bound by my spells and will never be found; I have destroyed you for Ra. The nine say: your head is split, Apep, your coils are slashed; you will never again wrap round Ra's boat; fire comes against you from the hidden place, and we have condemned you. They live on Ra's food, and offerings are made for them on earth.\n\nTo the second nine Tem says: you who have life and authority, drive the serpent Seba from Khuti and slash the evil serpent Af with knives. They bewitch Apep, open the earth to Ra and shut it against Apep at the gates of Khenti-Amenti. They praise Ra, protect him and rejoice at his enemy's fall; they lament when he has passed, for then darkness covers them and their circle is closed.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'by the lord of food (or, as lords of food)' is Budge's own alternative.",

  // DIVISION 4 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.4.gate":
    "Budge describes the third gate, built like the second. Nine mummy gods, 'the third company', guard its outer wall; a mummy god with a cobra on his brow stands at each end of the corridor, holding out his hands to Ra, and two serpents fill the corridor with fire 'for Ra'. The gateway is called Nebt-s-tchefau. The gods in it greet the great god: open the earth, force a way through the underworld and the region above, and drive away our darkness; hail Ra, come to us. The serpent Tchetbi guards the door. Sa tells it: open your gate to Ra and your doors to Khuti, so that he may send light into the darkness and make his light shine in the hidden home. The door shuts after him, and those in the gateway lament.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge's own errata to this volume (p. 119) correct the serpent's name from Tchetbi to Nebt-tchefau.",
  "egy.gates.4.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the fourth division. Four gods tow the boat to a long, low building with nine small shrines, in each of which a mummy god lies on his back: 'the gods who follow Osiris, in their abodes' (literally 'holes'). Before them two groups of six women, 'the hour goddesses in the underworld', stand on a slope half land and half water, divided by a many-coiled serpent, Hereret, which spawns twelve serpents for the hours to devour.\n\nRa says to his towers: pull me, beings of the underworld, for I made you; turn towards the east of heaven and the hidden mountain whose light goes round among the gods. To the lying gods he says: arise; I have set the plan of your existence, you in your tombs whose souls are broken, who live on your own filth; rise before my disk and set yourselves right by my rays. They lament when their doors close.\n\nTo the hour goddesses, who guide Ra straight with their instruments, he says: work, eat and rest in your gateways, breasts to the darkness and backs to the light; raise the serpent Hereret and live on what comes out of it; your duty is to eat its spawn and destroy it. Pull me, for I begot you. Take your rest, you Hours.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Ares (or, Sar)' and 'light (or, radiance)' are Budge's own alternatives.",
  "egy.gates.4.upper":
    "Budge describes one side of the fourth division: twelve bearded gods, 'who carry their doubles (ka)'; twelve jackal-headed gods around the 'Lake of Life'; and ten living cobras around the 'Lake of the Cobras'.\n\nThe first carry offerings to the god's home. Ra tells them: your task is to offer your doubles; you have your own offerings, your enemies are destroyed, your spirits are on their thrones and your souls in their places. They answer: praise to you, Ra-Khuti, soul protected in the earth, eternity, lord of years without end.\n\nThe jackals come out of a lake that the souls of the dead cannot approach because it is holy. Ra tells them to guard their lives and offerings at its edge. They say: bathe in your holy lake, Ra, where the lord of the gods bathed, which the dead cannot approach. Their drink includes wine.\n\nAt the cobras' speech, when Ra comes, souls are turned back and shadows destroyed. Ra tells them: guard your flames and fire to hurl at my enemies and at those whose mouths are evil. They answer: come to us and stride over Tanen.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The goddess Mu-sta, who supplies the first gods' food, is marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.gates.4.lower":
    "Budge describes the other side of the fourth division. Osiris Khenti-Amenti, mummy-shaped and wearing the crown of the South, stands on a serpent in a domed shrine, buried in a mountain up to his head. Before the shrine are a fire goddess in cobra form, twelve gods, and Horus the Elder, hawk-headed, leaning on a staff. Twelve gods stand behind the shrine; beyond them are four pits, each with a god bowing to a 'Master of Earths (?)'.\n\nHorus works magic for his father and gives him back his crown, saying: my heart goes out to you, father; you are avenged on those who worked against you; you rule, Khenti-Amenti; the spirits fear you and the dead are terrified; your crown is restored. The twelve gods say: let Khenti-Amenti be praised; your son Horus has restored your crown, protected you with magic and crushed your enemies. Osiris answers: come, my son Horus, avenge me on those who work against me and throw them to the one who guards the pits of destruction. Horus tells the gods behind the shrine to stand up, live on the bread of Hu and the ale of Maat, and smite his father's enemies, throwing them into the pits of fire as Ra has commanded.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Master of Earths (?)' and the word 'found (?)' in Horus's last speech are marked as doubtful by Budge.",

  // DIVISION 5 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.5.gate":
    "Budge describes the fourth gate, like the one before. Nine gods, 'the fourth company', guard it; a jackal-headed god stands at each end of the fire-swept corridor, holding out his hands to Ra. The gateway is called Arit. The nine gods say: Ra-Heru-Khuti opens our doors and our gateways; hail Ra, come to us, great god of hidden nature. The upright serpent Teka-hra guards the door. Sa tells it: open your gate to Ra and your doors to Khuti, so that he may send light into the darkness. The door shuts after him and those in the gateway lament. Budge does not repeat the hieroglyphic text, which is the same as at the previous gate.",
  "egy.gates.5.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the fifth division. Four gods tow the boat. Ahead, nine wrapped gods hold a long thin serpent, Ennutchi; ahead of them, twelve bearded men, 'the souls of the people in the underworld', approach a god called 'He of his corner'.\n\nRa tells his towers: pull me, sing my praises, you at the head of the stars; let your ropes be strong, your arms steady, your legs quick, your souls determined and your hearts glad; open a good way into the chambers of hidden things. Of the serpent-bearers it is said: the serpent goes as far as the gateway Nebt-ahau but no further. Ra tells them: strike the serpent Ennutchi, give it no way out, so that I may pass. They live by hearing his word.\n\nTo those who spoke what is right and true on earth and honoured the god's forms, Ra says: praise to your souls, breath to your nostrils, and you will have plots in the Field of Reeds (Sekhet-Aru); what belongs to the gods of truth is yours, and your homes are at the corner where those who weigh words live with me. Ra tells the god of the corner to call these true souls and seat them in peace in their homes.",
  "egy.gates.5.upper":
    "Budge describes one side of the fifth division: twelve men bowing in worship, 'those who praise in the underworld'; twelve men carrying a measuring cord, 'holders of the cord'; and four gods with life signs and sceptres.\n\nThe worshippers sang to Ra in the West and knew him and made him offerings on earth. They say: come, Ra, travel through the underworld and enter the holy places with the serpent Mehen. Ra answers: there are offerings for you who made offerings; I am content with what you did for me when I shone in the east and when I set in the chamber of my Eye.\n\nThe cord-holders measure the fields of the blessed spirits. Ra tells them: take the cord, pull it tight and mark out the fields of the West. Each spirit has been judged by the one in the cord: righteousness for those who exist, unrighteousness for those who do not. What is right is the cord in the West; your possessions and homesteads are yours. The gods are content with what they have and the spirits with their homes; their food comes from the Field of Reeds. Ra tells the four gods, the overseers of the cords, to give measured fields to the gods and spirits.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'limit (or, passage)', 'stretching (or, drawing)' and 'sand (?)' are Budge's own alternatives or doubts.",
  "egy.gates.5.lower":
    "Budge describes the other side of the fifth division: Horus leaning on a staff; four groups of four men, whom Budge explains as Egyptians (Reth), desert peoples to the east and north-east (Aamu), black Africans to the south (Nehesu) and fair-skinned Libyans (Themehu); twelve men holding a long serpent, 'holders of the span of time in the West'; and eight 'sovereign chiefs of the underworld'.\n\nHorus speaks to the creatures of Ra who live in Egypt and in the deserts on either side: protection to you, creatures of Ra who came from the Great One at the head of heaven; breath to your nostrils, and may your wrappings be loosened. Each people's name is explained by a pun on how it came into being: the Egyptians from the tears of the god's eye; the Aamu as 'mighty of issue', made by Sekhet; the Nehesu from the god's own seed, made by Horus; and the Themehu when the god sought his Eye, made by Sekhet. Each of these gods protects or avenges their souls.\n\nRa tells the holders of the serpent, which measures out the lifetimes of the souls in the West: destroy the souls of the enemies. The sovereign chiefs fix the length of life of the souls in the West and destroy the enemies; Horus says he has come to settle his account and shoot evils against his enemies.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'delivereth (or, avengeth)' and 'pass the edict concerning (literally, write)' are Budge's own alternatives.",

  // DIVISION 6 (Budge's chapters VII and VIII) --------------------------------
  "egy.gates.6.hall-1":
    "Budge describes the fifth gate, Nebt-aha, guarded by twelve mummy forms, with 'True of heart' and 'Hidden of heart' at the ends of the fire-swept corridor. The gods greet Ra: come to us, you at the head of the horizon; open the holy doors of the hidden place.\n\nBetween this gate and the sixth division is a remarkable scene, the Judgment Hall of Osiris. Under a roof edged with spear-head ornaments hang four upside-down animal heads. Osiris, in the double crown, holding life sign and sceptre, sits enthroned at the top of nine steps; on each step stands a god of 'the company with Osiris'. On the top step is a balance in which the deeds of the dead are weighed, held by a mummy-shaped figure: one pan holds something rectangular, the other a bird that stands for evil. Behind the balance a boat sails away from Osiris carrying a pig, driven by an ape with a stick; a second ape steers. Anubis is in one corner, and enemies of Osiris lie under the platform. A variant on the sarcophagus of Tchehra in Paris names the pig 'Eater of the Arm' and adds a man with a hatchet by the scales, probably ready to destroy the dead person if the verdict goes against him. A label over the hall, Budge says, means 'Osiris, governor of the underworld', with the last signs uncertain.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge says the end of the label over the hall is doubtful; Lefébure read it as 'Osiris, master of Hades, Earth, and Tanen'.",
  "egy.gates.6.hall-2":
    "Budge discusses the labels of the Judgment Hall, written in a cryptic ('enigmatic') script that Champollion first recognised; the Tchehra sarcophagus gives them in ordinary signs. One reads: his enemies are under his feet, the gods and spirits are before him; Osiris restrains his enemies, destroys and slaughters them. Budge quotes Goodwin's renderings: of Anubis, 'may Thoth weigh the words'; over the boat, 'when this god enters, the ape rises and restrains the Eater of the Arm'; behind the balance, 'the balance-bearer does homage... the morning star disperses the darkness; there is good will above, justice below', and a different version by Lefébure. Two more short labels are so difficult that scholars disagree on the order of their signs; comparing the tomb of Ramesses VI, Budge divides them into two and gives Lefébure's French-based renderings, one about the hidden elect and one about the weighing of words in the place of examination.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge gives two rival translations (Goodwin's and Lefébure's) of the legend behind the balance, and says the two upper legends are full of difficulty, so all these readings are tentative.",
  "egy.gates.6.gate":
    "Budge notes that the gateway into the sixth division has already been described; the upright serpent Set-em-maat-f guards the door. Sa tells it: open your gate to Ra and your doors to Khuti, so that he may send light into the darkness; the door shuts after him and those in the gateway lament. Budge explains that this division is incomplete on Seti I's sarcophagus, so he gives first the surviving fragments from the sarcophagus and then the complete scenes from the tomb of Ramesses VI as published by Lefébure.",
  "egy.gates.6.seti-middle":
    "From Seti I's sarcophagus, the middle band of the sixth division: two of the gods who tow the boat; the aged god Tem leaning on a staff; and a row of jackal-headed standards named after gods (Ra, Tem, Kheper, Shu, Seb, Osiris, Horus), each with two bound 'enemies', the damned, with the two Eyes keeping watch, mummy-shaped figures between the standards and a god with a sceptre.\n\nThe broken text: the towers say, rise up, disk; go to the standards of Seb. Tem tells the standards and the gods behind them: guard the enemies and bind those who are to be struck; I give you power to bind them; do not let them escape your hands or slip through your fingers. Enemies, you are counted for slaughter by the decree of the one who made the underworld with his own limbs; he has decreed your punishment and keeps count of you and of what you do.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'created the Tuat by his members (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and the text breaks off at several points.",
  "egy.gates.6.seti-upper":
    "From the badly damaged upper band of Seti I's sarcophagus: five men with loaves on their heads and six with the feather of Maat, originally twelve of each, as Champollion's copies show. Budge names the two groups Hetepti-kheperu and Autu-maamu-kheru-maat.\n\nThe surviving text: they offered incense to the gods and their doubles have been washed; they have been reckoned up and are found true before the great god who destroys wrongdoing. Osiris tells them: you are truth itself; be at peace for what you have done, you who follow me and live in the house of the one whose souls are holy; live on what is lived on there and rule the cool waters of your lake (the text then breaks off).\n\nBudge adds that some years before he had bought from a man at Luxor a fragment of the sarcophagus lid, now in the British Museum (no. 29948): it shows three men with loaves and a broken text saying their bread is ordered by their gods, their doubles are in their hands, and Osiris tells them their bread will come from what comes out of their own mouths.",
  "egy.gates.6.seti-lower":
    "From the lower band of Seti I's sarcophagus: five men (originally twelve) tending huge ears of grain, 'those who work the grain in the fields of the underworld', and a man with a sickle, one of seven reapers.\n\nThe text: they work the grain and embrace the grain god Nepra; their grain becomes glorious through the light of Ra when he appears and gives heat. The lord of joy of heart tells them: let your grain be glorious and your young shoots sprout; let the grain god grow and let Osiris be the food of the gods. They say to Ra: let the fields of the underworld be green; shine on the limbs of Osiris; when you shine the young plants come into being, creator of the grain.\n\nOf the reapers: they hold their sickles and reap their fields. Ra tells them: take your sickles and reap your grain, for it is granted to you; join me in the circle of hidden forms. Hail, reapers. They receive offerings on earth as holders of sickles in the fields of the underworld.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'which is eaten (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and several words are lost.",
  "egy.gates.6.rvi-middle":
    "The middle band of the sixth division as it appears in the tomb of Ramesses VI. The towers say: be exalted, disk (Aten), at the head of the light; look at the homes of the underworld; gods, watch Ra, the power in Akert; this great god decides your fates. He comes to the standards of Seb, which count up the enemies after the weighing of words in the West. Tem tells the standards: guard the enemies, bind those to be struck, let none escape your hands or slip through your fingers. Enemies, you are doomed by Ra's decree; his person is the body of Akert, and he made the underworld from his own frame; he has decreed your restraint and the doom that will be carried out on you in the great hall of Ra. The gods weep and lament; he sets them to guard you, and the enemies in the underworld are condemned to these standards.",
  "egy.gates.6.rvi-upper":
    "The upper band in the tomb of Ramesses VI: twelve 'gods of truth bearing truth' with feathers on their heads and twelve 'gods of offerings bearing provisions' with loaves.\n\nThe text: offerings of incense to their gods and cool water to their doubles. Osiris tells them: your bread is yours, according to what you said, with the cakes of Kheper; you will control your legs and be content in your hearts. They did what was right on earth and fought for their god, and they will be called to the land of the House of Life with truth; what is theirs by right will be given them before the great god who does away with wrongdoing. Osiris says to the gods of truth: truth be yours, and peace for what you did in following me, you who live in the house whose soul is holy; live on what those there live on and rule the cool waters of your land; I have decreed that you shall exist there in truth and without sin. Their bread is truth-cakes and their drink wine.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'without sin (or, defects)' is Budge's own alternative, and several words are lost.",
  "egy.gates.6.rvi-lower":
    "The lower band in the tomb of Ramesses VI: twelve men tending enormous ears of grain (or trees), overseen by a god leaning on a staff, and a group of reapers with sickles.\n\nThe damaged text: they work the grain and cultivate the divine grain; the spirits eat their grain in the land of the god of light, who comes and passes by them. The lord of joy of heart tells them: let your grain be glorious and your ears of wheat sprout, and let your offerings be for Ra; your cakes are in the underworld and the offerings that are yours by right are decreed for you. They say to Ra: let plants spring up in the fields of the underworld; shine on the limbs of Osiris; when you shine the young plants come into being, creator of the Egg. To the reapers Ra says: take your sickles and reap your grain, for it is granted to you; join the circle of the hidden of forms. Hail, reapers.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge marks 'ear of corn (?)' and 'decreed (?)' as doubtful, and glosses 'the divine grain' also as the god Nepra.",

  // DIVISION 7 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.7.gate":
    "Budge describes the sixth gate, Pestit, like the one before: nine gods, 'the seventh company', guard it, and a bearded god with hidden arms stands at each end of the fire-swept corridor, holding out his hands to Ra. The gods acclaim the great god as he enters; what survives of their speech says: open the secret places, open the holy gateways and unfold the hidden doors. The upright serpent Akha-en-maat guards the door, and Sa tells it: open your gate to Ra and your doors to Khuti, so that he may send light into the darkness. The door shuts after him and those in the gateway lament. Budge says the damaged part of this text can be restored with certainty.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The name of the god at the corridor's exit (Heqes) is marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.gates.7.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the seventh division. Four gods tow Ra's boat, saying: you are towed along, great god, lord of the hours, who work for those under the earth. Ra answers: magic protection and holiness to you who tow me into the deepest parts of the underworld; tow me to the chambers and stand on the hidden mountain of the horizon.\n\nAhead march twelve gods with hidden arms, 'who hold the mystery' of the great god. The dead who burn in Het-Benben, the temple of Ra, see him and come to where his body is. Ra tells them: receive my forms and embrace your hidden forms; the hiddenness in you is that of the underworld; cover your arms with it. They answer: let your soul be in heaven, your shadow enter the hidden place and your body be in the earth.\n\nThen come eight 'gods of the temples' and eight gods with arms held out, who stand outside Het-Benben, see Ra and bring the offerings. Ra says his provisions come from theirs, and he guards his secret things in Het-Benben. The god Tuati tells the gods who lie on their own land: your flesh is yours; you have gathered your bones, knit your limbs and collected your flesh; sweet air is at your nostrils, and you have put on your clothes and your wigs.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the chambers (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and 'possess the hiddenness (or, hold the mystery)' is his own alternative.",
  "egy.gates.7.upper":
    "Budge describes the upper band. Twelve gods hold forked weapons (metau). Ra tells them: take your weapons and gash the serpent fiend Mamu when heads appear out of him, and turn him back. They answer: our weapons are for Ra against Mamu; we will gash the great evil worm. They travel in Ra's boat and drive back Apep in the sky and in the West.\n\nTwelve gods hold up the serpent Seba-Apep, from whose head and back grow twelve human heads. Ra tells them: turn back Seba, drive Apep backwards when the heads appear, and let him perish. He tells the heads they will be eaten as they come out and must go back into the coils. The worm Hefau will be without eyes, nose and ears and will live only on his own roaring.\n\nLast, twelve men hold a double rope tied to the neck of a mummy figure, Qan (also written Aqen), each standing in a loop of the rope with a star before him. The text: the enemy of Ra comes out of the underworld; seize the rope and tie up the mouth of Aqen; your hours come out, and there is benefit for you in them. When Ra calls, the rope stays in place and puts an end to him. They say: the god is tied up with the rope; the hours are yours, Ra, with light.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the hours of the gods (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and the bound figure's name is spelled several ways (Qan, Aqen, Aneq, Naq) in his text.",
  "egy.gates.7.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band: the god Tuati leaning on a staff; the serpent Nehep, whose long body, on twenty-four lion's legs, serves as a bier for twelve sleeping mummy gods, 'those in the body of Osiris asleep'; four gods with arms held out; four more gods; a serpent inside a circular pool; and a mummy god.\n\nTuati tells the sleepers: lift up your flesh, gather your bones, gird your limbs and bring your flesh together; there is fresh air for your nostrils. Loosen your wrappings, take off your wigs, open your eyes and look at the light; rise up from your helpless state and take your fields in the 'Field, lord of offerings'. The serpent Nehep gives them their bodies and souls; they go on to the Field of Reeds, control their libations and walk on the earth.\n\nOf the pool: a serpent lives in it, its water is fire, and the gods and souls of the earth cannot go down to it because of the serpent's flames. Ra tells its guardians: the water of this pool is Osiris; its flame destroys the souls that dare to approach him, and the dread of it cannot be overcome. Budge's text then repeats the passage about the fields and the pool, and breaks off.",

  // DIVISION 8 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.8.gate":
    "Budge describes the seventh gate, Bekhkhi, which leads to the eighth division. Nine mummy gods, 'the company of nine', guard its outwork; at the ends of the fire-swept corridor stand two mummy forms with folded hands, who hold out their hands to Ra. The gods say: come to us, you at the head of the horizon, great god who opens hidden places; open the holy gateways and their doors. The upright serpent Set-hra guards the door; Sa tells it: open your gate to Ra and your doorway to Khuti, so that he may light the darkness and send light into the hidden home. The gate shuts after him and the souls on the other side wail when they hear it close.",
  "egy.gates.8.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the eighth division. Four gods tow the boat, led by an aged god leaning on a staff, 'He who lives in Nu' (the primeval water). Before them a long tank holds four groups of four figures in the water, which Budge translates as 'bathers, floaters, swimmers and divers'.\n\nThe towers say: praise in heaven to the soul of Ra and on earth to his body; heaven is made young by his soul and earth by his body; we open the hidden place for you and straighten the roads; you come out to those who are in the water.\n\nThe aged god tells those in the water: look at Ra in his boat, great of mystery, who orders the fates of the gods and plans for the spirits; rise up and pay heed to him. Ra says to them: put out your heads, you who are in the water; stretch out your arms and legs; breath to your nostrils. You will rule your waters, be at peace in your cool pools, pass through the waters of Nu; your souls are on earth and will be satisfied and not destroyed.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'He (literally, those) who is in Nu' is Budge's own note, and 'destinies (or, affairs)' and 'performeth (or, maketh)' are his alternatives.",
  "egy.gates.8.upper":
    "Budge describes the upper band: twelve gods, the officials who give allotted bread and green plants to the souls in the Lake of Serser ('blazing fire'); nine human-headed hawks with hands raised in praise, each with bread and green plants before him, 'the souls in the Lake of Serser'; and a god holding a sceptre.\n\nRa tells the officials: give your appointed bread and bring your green plants to the souls who are ordered to exist in the Lake of Serser. They answer that it is done: the way is fair, Khenti-Amenti praises you and so do those in Ta-thenen.\n\nThe souls have received their bread and rule this lake. Ra tells them: eat your green plants, be satisfied with your cakes, let your bellies be full and your hearts content; your plants are from the Lake of Serser, which may not be approached; praise me, for I am the Great One of terror of the underworld. They answer: hail to you, great one of the Powers; the underworld is yours, a hidden place you made for those in its circles; the height of heaven is yours; the earth is for your dead body and the sky for your soul; be at peace with what you have made.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'by those who are with (?) Tui' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and 'be at peace (or, be content)' is his own alternative.",
  "egy.gates.8.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band: Horus the Elder leaning on a staff; twelve 'burnt enemies of Osiris', bound in three different painful ways; and a great speckled serpent, Kheti, lying in coils in front of them and breathing fire into their leader's face, with a mummy god standing in each coil.\n\nThe text: this is what Horus does for his father Osiris. He tells the enemies: fetters on your arms, enemies of my father; your arms tied up towards your heads, or behind you, rebels against Ra. You will be hacked in pieces and will never exist again; your souls will be destroyed because of what you did to my father Osiris: you threw his mysteries behind your backs and dragged his statue out of the secret place, laying bare the hidden things of the resting place of the Great One who begot me. My father's word is true against you, and so is mine; you will cease to exist.\n\nHorus says: my serpent Kheti, mighty fire, from whose mouth comes the flame that is in my Eye, whose coils my children guard, open your mouth and breathe fire against my father's enemies; burn their bodies and their souls. Whoever knows the spells against this serpent will not walk on his fiery path. Budge completes the defective end from the tomb of Ramesses VI: offerings are made to the gods on the serpent.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'desecrated (literally, laid bare)' and 'rest (or, resting-place)' are Budge's own alternatives.",

  // DIVISION 9 --------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.9.gate":
    "Budge describes the eighth gate, Aat-shefsheft, leading to the ninth division. The gods in it acclaim the great god as he enters. Nine mummy gods guard the outwork; two mummy forms stand at the ends of the corridor, which two cobras sweep with fire. The gods say: come to us, you at the head of the horizon, great god who opens the secret places; open the holy gateways and unfold their holy doors. The upright serpent Ab-ta guards the door; Sa tells it to open the gate to Ra and the doorway to Khuti, so that he may light the darkness and send light into the hidden home. The gate shuts after him and the souls on the other side wail.",
  "egy.gates.9.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the ninth division. Four gods tow the boat. Ahead go six men, four apes and four women, each group holding a bent rod or rope over their heads, 'who work magic by knots for Ra'; three spearmen; and a man with a sun disk and ass's ears lying face down, 'the Ass', held by a rope the spearmen grasp. Facing him are the serpent Apep and the crocodile Shesshes, whose tail ends in a serpent's head.\n\nThe towers say: the god comes to his body and is towed to his shadow; be at peace with your body, Ra; we will tow you whole into your secret place. The magicians march before Ra, speak spells against Apep, rise with him into the height of heaven and make him rise in the sky. Their spell: away with you, rebel serpent, destroyer, Apep who sends out evil; your face will be destroyed; you go to the block of execution; the spearmen will drive their harpoons into you and bewitch you; you are destroyed, smashed and stabbed to death. The spearmen guard the Ass's rope and keep the worm away from the boat. Their own speech is missing.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'those who are over the curved ropes (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and a word is lost in 'by means of their ...'.",
  "egy.gates.9.upper":
    "Budge describes the upper band. Four gods whose heads are crowns of the South with cobras, helped by a bearded man, raise on a rope a pole topped by a head in the crown of the South: 'the gods of the South'. Four with crowns of the North do the same: 'the gods of the North'. Between them is a hawk-headed sphinx, 'Horus in the Boat', from whose back rises a man called Ana; on it stands the two-headed Horus-Set, laying his hands on the crowns. Budge concludes that Horus was seen as a form of the sun god of the South and Set of the North. Then come the serpent Shemti, with four heads and legs at each end, held by Apu; the serpent Bata, with a crowned head at each end, carrying another serpent from which eight half-figures rise; and two men swinging a net.\n\nRa tells the gods of the South: take your heads, pull tight your rope; come into being and have the power of light, by my coming into being in the secret place. Horus-Set, 'this god with two faces', goes in after Ra has passed. Ra tells the gods of the North: take your crowns and pull tight; behold Horus of the handsome face. Shemti and Bata stride through the secret place; those in them are heads that have been devoured. The net-holders use spells for Horus-Ra in the West on those in their nets.",
  "egy.gates.9.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band. Sixteen beings hold a rope: four 'souls of the West', four ibis-headed 'followers of Thoth', four hawk-headed 'followers of Horus' and four ram-headed 'followers of Ra'. At the other end eight 'Powers' hold another rope. Both ropes are tied to the legs of the great serpent Khepri, which has a head at each end and stands on human legs; on its middle coil sits a hawk in the double crown, 'Horus of the underworld'.\n\nThe text: they pull the rope, tied to Khepri, who moves backwards to the hall of their horizon, and tow the god into the sky. They live on the things of the South and the North and on what comes from Ra's mouth. They say to Ra: come after your transformations; appear in heaven, in the great heaven; we decree your homes. Of Horus: the head comes out of him; Ra calls to this god, to whom the two cobras join themselves, and he goes in on the way into Khepri. The eight Powers say: the ways of the hidden place are open to you, the doors in the earth are unfolded for you, soul whom Nut loves; we will guide your wings to the mountain; enter the East and pass out between the thighs of your mother.",

  // DIVISION 10 -------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.10.gate":
    "Budge describes the ninth gate, Tcheserit, leading to the tenth division. Sixteen cobras guard its outwork; a mummy form with a knife stands at each end of the fire-swept corridor. The cobras say: come to us, you at the head of the horizon, great god who opens the secret place; open the holy gateways and unfold the doors of the earth. The upright serpent Sethu guards the door; Sa tells it to open its gate and doorway so that the god may light the darkness and the hidden home. The gate closes after him and the cobras on the other side wail.",
  "egy.gates.10.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the tenth division. Four gods tow the boat. Ahead go the god of the hour holding two stars; four kneeling gods with cobras over their heads (Horus, Sereq, Abesh and lion-headed Sekhet); three star gods towing a small boat that carries a cobra encircling the 'Face of the Disk'; a winged serpent; a god catching fire from a staff; a serpent with two mummy figures on its neck; four women 'Criers'; and two bows set end to end, bearing cobras, on which stands the two-headed Horus-Set.\n\nThe towers say: we tow Ra into the sky; take hold of your Face, Ra, and unite with it; let the two Eyes of Khuti enter it and drive the darkness from the West. The god of the hour lights the upper heaven and leads the hour. The kneeling gods sit on great images beneath them and pass on with Ra. The star gods sing with their stars, grasp the ropes of their boat, and the Face of Ra sails into the sky. The winged serpent guides the god to the eastern horizon. With the cobra goes 'the establishing of time reckoned in writing by years'. The Criers call: enter, Ra; come, you born of the underworld, offspring of the heights of heaven. The bows are the serpent Mehen of the cobras, which carry the Two-Faced one and lead Ra into the eastern sky.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'he maketh a rising up for Ra (or, he stablisheth Ra)' and the description of the fire-god ('placeth fire on the head and horns', or 'in his hands is the fire') are Budge's alternatives.",
  "egy.gates.10.upper":
    "Budge describes the upper band. Eight gods armed with knives and curved staffs (four Antiu and four Henatiu, the latter with four cobras for heads). Then the long serpent Apep, whose voice goes round the underworld, with a great chain fixed to its neck; the chain passes through the hands of sixteen men, is held down by a great hand, 'Hidden Body', and runs into the earth before Khenti-Amenti. The goddess Serq lies on the chain. Five gods, Seb and the four sons of Horus, lean on its second curve, each holding a serpent in fetters.\n\nThe armed gods say: rise, Ra; be strong, Khuti; we will overthrow Apep in his fetters; Apep is stabbed with knives; Ra will stand up in the hour of peace and pass on when his chain is fixed. The boat moves towards Apep's region and goes on once he is restrained. Four gods say: come forward, Ra; fetters have been laid on Neha-hra and Apep is in his bonds. Twelve guard the deadly chain held by the Hidden Hand and say: darkness on your face, Uamemti; you sons of the helpless one will be destroyed by the Hidden Hand; Seb guards your fetters and the sons of Horus put the chain on you. The sons of Horus make the fetters heavy, and the boat of the Well-doing God travels on.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'rise up (or, stand)', 'the hour wherein he is content (or, the hour of peace)' and 'reptile (literally, worm)' are Budge's own alternatives.",
  "egy.gates.10.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band: twelve gods with paddles, 'gods who never diminish'; twelve goddesses of the hours, each with a star, holding the rope that tows Ra's boat; an ape-headed god, a god with a star, a bull-headed god and another star god, each with a sceptre; a monkey on a bracket, 'the god of Rethenu' (Syria); the Eye of Ra on a bracket; and a god called Her-nest-f.\n\nThe paddlers come into being of themselves whenever Ra is born in the sky; they carry him once he is in the eastern horizon. Ra tells them: take your paddles and join your stars; you come into being when I do and are born when I am born; you will not diminish. The hour goddesses tow him into the sky and guide him along its roads; he tells them: take the rope, take your positions, pull me into the height of heaven; my births make you be born; establish the periods of time and years.\n\nFour short texts say the four gods with sceptres call on the gateways to open, call to the stars about the god's births, call to the gods of the boat, and set the stars in their places. Budge adds two lines from the tomb of Ramesses VI: the Eye of Ra rejoices in its place in the boat, and the door-opener of this circle stays where he is and does not go on with Ra.",

  // DIVISION 11 -------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.11.gate":
    "Budge describes the tenth gate, Shetat-besu, leading to the eleventh division. Two mummy forms stand at the ends of the fire-swept corridor. Where gods usually stand there are two sceptres topped by White Crowns, the symbols of Osiris and Horus, with a line of text between them: they say to Ra, come in peace, come in peace; you of many transformations, your soul is in heaven and your body in the earth; it is your own command, great one. The upright serpent Am-netu-f guards the door; Sa tells it to open the gate and doorway so that the god may light the darkness and the hidden home. The gate closes after him and the gods on the battlements wail.",
  "egy.gates.11.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle of the eleventh division. Four gods tow the boat. Ahead go nine gods with knives and sceptres, 'the company who slay Apep'; the serpent Apep, held by five chains pegged into the ground, which represent the children of Horus; four apes each holding up a huge hand; the goddesses of Upper and Lower Egypt; and a god Sebekhti.\n\nThe towers say: behold Ra coming out of the West and taking his place in the two parts of Nu, changing his form on the two hands of Nu. He does not enter the height of heaven, but opens a way through the underworld into it by his transformations in Nu. What opens the underworld into the sky is the two hands of 'He whose name is hidden'; he exists in thick darkness, and light appears from the starry night.\n\nThe nine hack Apep with knives, slaughter him and drive in stakes to fetter him; the children of Horus hold his fetters between their fingers. While his coils are in the sky, his poison drips down into the West. The apes prepare Ra's way into the eastern horizon, two on each side, praise his soul and establish his disk. The last three turn Set away from this gate, open its cavern and secure the hidden gateways.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'whilst his folds (?) are in the sky' is marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.gates.11.upper":
    "Budge describes the upper band: four gods holding disks, four holding stars, four with sceptres 'who come forth', four ram-headed and four hawk-headed gods with sceptres, eight goddesses seated on coiled cobras each holding a star, 'the protecting hours', and a crocodile-headed god, Sebek-Ra, holding a serpent.\n\nThe disk-bearers carry the disk of Ra and open a way through the underworld into the height of heaven; they speak to the gateway of Akert so that Ra may set himself in the body of Nut, the sky. The star-bearers shout hymns when the arms of Nu embrace Ra and go with him into the sky. The sceptre-bearers establish the god's domains in the sky. The ram-headed gods decree the gods' offerings from the bread of heaven and bring out the water of heaven before Ra has come out of Nu. The hawk-headed gods set up the shrine in Ra's boat, lay hands on it as it comes out of the gate, and place its paddles in the sky when one hour begins and the last goes to rest. The goddesses on cobras come out four to the east and four to the west, call the spirits of the East, sing to the god and guide those in his boat. Budge notes there is no text for Sebek-Ra.",
  "egy.gates.11.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band: groups of four: gods in the crown of the South, 'Weepers', gods in the crown of the North, 'those who give names', goddesses in the crown of the South and of the North, goddesses without crowns, and bowing gods who praise Ra; and a cat-headed god, Mati.\n\nThe texts say: the crowned gods set the White Crown on Ra's followers; they stay in the underworld but their souls go forward and stand at this gate. The Weepers lament for Osiris after Ra has left the West; their souls follow Ra but they themselves follow Osiris. Others bring about Ra's births in the earth, or give names to Ra and magnify the names of all his forms; their souls follow him while their bodies stay in their places. The goddesses of the South make truth (maat) advance and set it in Ra's shrine when he takes his place in the sky; those of the North establish time and make the years. The uncrowned women lament and tear their hair before the great god in the West; they make Set withdraw from this gateway and do not go into the sky. The bowing gods praise Ra and the gods who guard the Hidden Door; they stay in their places, as does the warder of this circle's door.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'places (or, seats)' is Budge's own alternative.",

  // DIVISION 12 -------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.gates.12.gate":
    "Budge describes the last gate, Tesert-baiu, through which the sun passes before coming out into heaven in the light of a new day. The gods in it acclaim the great god. Instead of the usual guardian gods there are two staffs topped by bearded heads, one with the disk of Tem and one with a beetle, the sign of Khepera: they stand up on their heads and come into being on their staffs by the gate. The two doors are guarded by two upright serpents, Sebi and Reri; Sa tells each to open its gate so that Ra may come out of the hidden place into the body of Nut; the souls in the West wail when the door closes. Two cobras, Isis and Nephthys, guard this hidden gate and follow the god.\n\nHere the underworld ends. The boat is lifted out of the waters by the two arms of Nu himself: 'these two arms come out of the waters and lift up this god'. The god appears in the boat as a beetle rolling a disk, with Isis and Nephthys beside him and Seb, Shu, Heka, Hu and Sa among the crew; the text says he takes his place in the morning boat. Beyond, a god bends his body into a ring so that his toes touch the back of his head: this is Osiris, 'whose circuit is the underworld'. On his head stands Nut, arms raised to receive the sun's disk, which the beetle rolls towards her: 'Nut receives Ra'.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge says the three beings at the front of the boat are 'probably' personifications of doors; the identification is his guess.",
};
